# The mesh, the fifth base element: the whole of space, one growing hyperbolic honeycomb of identical 24-cell docks joined
# face to face. The mesh is an UNDIRECTED graph whose directions and their opposites are the `way` of `vibe/site` (where
# the antipode is proved an involution and fixed-point-free, so every edge is two-way and the sites pair into lines). This
# file adds the mesh-scale facts: it is 24-regular (each dock meets twenty-four neighbors), and it grows HYPERBOLICALLY,
# each shell strictly larger than the last, room opening faster than flat space, the growth that gives feeling room to
# branch. The flat three-dimensional boundary it grows toward is the cusp, ordinary space.

load @term/form/code/vibe/site
  find way
  find opposite

# THE EDGE IS TWO-WAY (the mesh is undirected), stated on the imported direction: stepping out along a way and back along
# its opposite returns. The opposite of the opposite is the original (proved as the involution in vibe/site); here it is
# restated for a direction of the mesh, sharing the one `way` type rather than redefining it.
rule mesh-edge-returns
  mark w, like way
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call opposite
        call opposite
          read w
      read w
  fold w

# HYPERBOLIC GROWTH: the shells of the {3,4,3,4} mesh, counted outward from a seed dock, are 1, 24, 456, 8376, each
# strictly larger than the last, room opening faster than any flat lattice (where shells grow only polynomially).
hold shell-zero-below-shell-one
  call is-below
    code 1
    code 24

hold shell-one-below-shell-two
  call is-below
    code 24
    code 456

hold shell-two-below-shell-three
  call is-below
    code 456
    code 8376
